{
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "concern",
      "claim": "The spec's own stated base does not match the snapshot this review is reading, and several sentences built on that premise are false here. depends_on: [SA-0280, SA-0282, SA-0283, ...] is correctly declared, and the spec repeatedly says it 'waits for three parents' and 'runs only once SA-0280, SA-0282 and SA-0283 are merged and retired to done/.' At the literal commit this review reads (9c27b82e), that is not yet true: SA-0280, SA-0282, SA-0283 are still queued top-level specs in .saffron/specs/, not in done/. Every sentence built on the opposite assumption is therefore false right now: 'Your base has the three parents in done/' (Notes); 'On the base this spec requires, SA-0280, SA-0282 and SA-0283 are retired as well, which makes ten' (Context) -- at this base it is seven, not ten; and, concretely, 'The queue smoke test promotes SA-0280, SA-0282 and SA-0283 from its measured set at your base. Move each one it lists as a candidate to its refusals' (Problem) -- verified against tests/test_scheduler.py::test_saffron_queue_smoke_reproduces_this_repos_measured_queue, at this base all three ids are already in that test's refusals list, not its candidates list (refused today for an unrelated depends_on reason, since their own dependency chains -- SA-0278, SA-0281, SA-0271, SA-0262, SA-0264 -- are likewise unmerged here). A cell handed this spec against this exact snapshot would find nothing to 'move,' and would find only seven retired .claude-touching specs rather than the ten it's told to hardcode. This resolves itself once SA-0280/SA-0282/SA-0283 actually land, and the standing depends_on gate is exactly the mechanism meant to prevent this spec from reaching a cell before then, so this is not marked a blocker. But a person should explicitly confirm, before any batch runs this spec, that it is not dispatched ahead of those three parents actually retiring (no manual override of depends_on, no --stack scheduling that bypasses the gate), because several of the literal instructions in Problem and Context are false exactly as written against the commit in front of us.",
      "criterion": null,
      "file": null,
      "line": null,
      "fixes": null
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "claim": "Several file:line citations in Context are stale relative to this base, though the content they describe is accurate. The Context section says 'Every line number below was read at cad84298.' That commit does not exist anywhere in this repository (git cat-file -t cad84298 and git rev-list --all both come up empty). The actual numbers trace to a real, different commit, 92d84998 ('spec(SA-0272)...', 2026-10-11), about 215 commits before this base, where protected_touch_refusal really is at saffron/scheduler.py:365-434, RefusalKind really is at 152-161, the _refuse call under protected_touch really is near 737-742, cli.py's collision/return-1 block really is at 533-536, and test_no_real_spec_is_refused_on_its_own_acceptance_criteria really sits at tests/test_scheduler.py:3083 with its depends_on assertions near 3107-3109. At the actual base (9c27b82e) these have all drifted: protected_touch_refusal is now at 392-461, that same test is now at 3634 with its assertions at 3651-3660, cli.py's block is now at 579-581. In every case checked, the content matches what's described, so this doesn't drive a wrong build, since an implementer who searches by function/test name lands in the right place regardless. But the named source commit is unverifiable, and a reader who jumps straight to the cited ranges lands on unrelated text (e.g. line 3083 today is mid-paragraph about SA-0114/SA-0115, not the retired-corpus test). Worth a by-hand touch-up, not worth blocking on.",
      "criterion": null,
